meta {
  name: What this caller may do
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/capabilities
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.service: eq core
  res.body.count: gte 1
  res.body.capabilities: isArray
  res.body.capabilities[0].key: isDefined
  res.body.capabilities[0].area: isDefined
  res.body.capabilities[0].action: isDefined
  res.body.capabilities[0].breadth: isDefined
  res.body.capabilities[0].fields.all: isDefined
}

docs {
  The other half of this folder's question. Every other request here asks what
  a caller can see by asking for rows and counting what comes back; this one
  asks the authorization layer directly, and it is what a client reads at
  startup to decide which buttons to draw.

  It is a tenant-plane route despite having nothing tenant-shaped in it.
  Without X-School-Id it is 400, not 401, because capabilities are answered
  per school - the same person is an office clerk at one branch's school and a
  parent at another.

  The count is not a fixed number. It is every entity in the authorization
  catalogue this caller holds, so it grows whenever a capability is added to
  the catalogue - which is why nothing here asserts on it beyond being
  non-empty. On a stock checkout with the development subject it came back
  130.

  WHAT THIS CANNOT TELL YOU UNDER THIS COLLECTION'S USUAL SETUP. With
  CAS_BASE_URL empty the guard is allow-all, so every entity is held, `reach`
  is absent from every entry and `breadth` is only ever school_wide or
  not_applicable. Nothing is narrowed, so the interesting part of the answer -
  which grants are section-scoped, which are pinned to particular rows -
  cannot appear. Point the API at real CAS and the same request is the fastest
  way to see what a token actually carries.

  breadth is derived, not stored: an action ending _all is school_wide, an
  action whose _all counterpart is also held is narrowed, and one with no _all
  counterpart in the catalogue at all is not_applicable. That last case is
  real and easy to misread - core.calendar.read has no core.calendar.read_all,
  so it reports not_applicable rather than school_wide, while
  core.timetable.read alongside core.timetable.read_all does not.

  row_pinned says the grant carries row constraints; fields says which columns
  survive projection, with all true meaning no column was withheld.
}
